1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:03,492
A script that does one thing once is just a command.

2
00:00:03,512 --> 00:00:07,441
A loop lets it do the same thing for a hundred files, in a second.

3
00:00:07,911 --> 00:00:08,747
In this video:

4
00:00:08,767 --> 00:00:13,267
use looping constructs, like for, to process files and command line input.

5
00:00:17,787 --> 00:00:20,370
Here's the task, the way an exam would state it.

6
00:00:20,840 --> 00:00:25,236
Use Looping constructs (for, etc.) to process file, command line input.

7
00:00:25,706 --> 00:00:29,312
We'll loop over a list, count with a range, loop over files,

8
00:00:29,332 --> 00:00:33,664
loop over the arguments given to a script, and read a file line by line.

9
00:00:43,184 --> 00:00:46,953
A loop repeats commands. The for loop takes a list of items.

10
00:00:46,973 --> 00:00:49,984
For each item, it puts the item into a variable,

11
00:00:50,004 --> 00:00:52,636
and runs the commands between do and done.

12
00:00:59,106 --> 00:01:03,473
The while loop is different: it keeps going as long as a command succeeds.

13
00:01:03,493 --> 00:01:06,318
We'll use it to read a file, one line at a time.

14
00:01:13,838 --> 00:01:17,138
Our first loop. for fruit in apple banana cherry.

15
00:01:17,158 --> 00:01:22,286
The variable fruit takes each word in turn. Between do and done, we echo it.

16
00:01:22,306 --> 00:01:25,741
A dollar sign in front of the name gives its value.

17
00:01:25,761 --> 00:01:31,567
The lab created this file; and we make it executable with the same chmod +x as before.

18
00:01:36,037 --> 00:01:39,260
It runs the loop three times: one line for each fruit.

19
00:01:44,780 --> 00:01:49,701
Counting. Curly braces with two dots expand to every number in between:

20
00:01:49,721 --> 00:01:53,738
1 to 5. echo shows the expansion; the shell does the work.

21
00:01:53,758 --> 00:01:56,173
The same range works in a for loop.

22
00:02:00,643 --> 00:02:03,013
The loop prints Number 1 through Number 5.

23
00:02:08,533 --> 00:02:14,750
Looping over files. *.txt is a wildcard: every file whose name ends in .txt.

24
00:02:14,770 --> 00:02:20,822
ls shows three of them. The loop puts each file name in the variable file.

25
00:02:26,342 --> 00:02:30,240
It runs once per file. wc -l counts the lines in a file:

26
00:02:30,260 --> 00:02:33,108
it prints the number, then the file name.

27
00:02:33,128 --> 00:02:37,586
The quotes around the variable keep file names with spaces safe.

28
00:02:43,106 --> 00:02:49,205
Command line input. Inside a script, "$@" means: every argument given to the script.

29
00:02:49,225 --> 00:02:52,046
The loop goes through them, one by one.

30
00:02:55,516 --> 00:02:57,975
We run it with three names: three greetings.

31
00:02:57,995 --> 00:03:01,469
With no names, the loop has nothing to do, and prints nothing.

32
00:03:07,989 --> 00:03:11,175
To process a file line by line, use while read.

33
00:03:11,195 --> 00:03:16,904
read -r takes one line into the variable line; the -r keeps backslashes as they are.

34
00:03:16,924 --> 00:03:22,497
The less-than sign feeds the file into the loop. The loop ends when the file does.

35
00:03:26,967 --> 00:03:31,150
colors.txt has three lines, and the loop reads three lines.

36
00:03:36,670 --> 00:03:40,106
Our five steps: task, demo, check, reboot, and your turn.

37
00:03:41,626 --> 00:03:44,684
Scripts are files on disk, so they survive a reboot.

38
00:03:44,704 --> 00:03:47,110
We restart the lab, and it comes back up.

39
00:03:50,580 --> 00:03:54,699
After a fresh boot, ./loop1.sh runs the same way.

40
00:04:02,219 --> 00:04:04,916
Your turn. Make a folder called lab17.

41
00:04:04,936 --> 00:04:10,922
In it, create an executable script, hello.sh, that greets every argument with Hello,

42
00:04:10,942 --> 00:04:15,212
a comma, and the name. With no arguments, it prints nothing.

43
00:04:21,682 --> 00:04:24,500
Run the checker, and try before you open the solution.

44
00:04:30,020 --> 00:04:33,797
Next up: process script inputs, like $1 and $2.

45
00:04:34,267 --> 00:04:37,170
Subscribe to follow the series, and let's keep going.
